    <item>
      <title>The Fading Light of Wisdom</title>
      <description>Today&apos;s story will change how you see everything... In the great war of Kurukshetra, the Pandavas and Kauravas clash on the sacred plain. Among the Kauravas is a mighty warrior named Duryodhana, son of the blind king Dhritarashtra. He has always been consumed by envy of his cousins, the Pandavas, and their wise leader Arjuna. As the war rages on, Duryodhana begins to tire of the endless fighting. He grows weary of the endless slaughter, the death and destruction. The sound of clashing steel and the stench of blood begin to repel him. He starts to question the true nature of victory. He starts to wonder if there&apos;s more to life than mere conquest. Duryodhana begins to withdraw from the battlefield, leaving his comrades to fight on. He retires to his chambers, away from the chaos. He meditates on the fleeting nature of life and the futility of war. He seeks the counsel of the wise sage, Vidura. But as he listens to Vidura&apos;s words, he finds himself growing more and more detached from the world around him. He starts to see the universe as a mere illusion, a dream from which he longs to awaken. And so he succumbs to the sin of Sloth, becoming apathetic and disconnected from the world. As the war rages on, Duryodhana&apos;s detachment grows, and with it, his power and influence wane. This is Sloth — the failure to engage with the world around us, to find meaning and purpose in our lives. Today, we retreat into our screens, our comforts, our complacency — ask yourself, are you awake, or are you asleep?</description>
      <pubDate>Tue, 07 Apr 2026 14:00:00 +0000</pubDate>
